  "query_preview": "..For Today and Thursday...High pressure will \ngradually build back over Arizona with the high pressure center\nbecoming established over the California/Arizona border by\nThursday. Under this pattern monsoon moisture will continue to \nlinger over and south of the Mogollon Rim and White Mountains \nbut further north a drier northwest flow will develop. As a \nresult, look for isolated afternoon and evening showers and \nthunderstorms along the Mogollon Rim and across the White \nMountains region. In addition, a weak moisture surge south of the \nMogollon Rim has upped the chances for a stray shower or storm \ntoday over the mountain areas of Yavapai and Gila Counties, which \ncould linger into Thursday. Little to no activity is anticipated\nnorth of the Mogollon Rim. Look for warmer temperatures as the \nhigh builds with mountain highs climbing to the upper 70s to mid \n80s and reaching 95 to 105 at lower elevations.\n\nFor Friday and the weekend..A trough is expected to dive across \nthe Pacific Northwest pushing the California/Arizona high center \neastward. It appears monsoon moisture will interact with the \npassing Pacific Northwest trough. The models are showing good \nchances for widespread coverage of showers and thunderstorms \nFriday and Friday night with diminishing chances from Saturday \ninto Sunday as the trough moves eastward. Vertical wind shear will\nstrengthen some associated with the passing trough. This usually \nmeans a few storms will become organized with isolated severe \nthunderstorms and localized flash flooding possible. Under the \ninfluence of the trough it will become breezy with general west to\nsouthwest winds at 10-20 mph gusting to 25-30 mph starting Friday\nand lasting through the weekend.\n\nLooking at early next week...The Pacific Northwest trough moves to\nthe east while an additional trough dives southward just off the\nwest coast of the United States. This will set up a deep south to\nsouthwest flow over Arizona between the west coast trough and \nhigh pressure over the central Unites States. The question, will \ndeep southerly flow develop with monsoon moisture pushing back \nacross all of Arizona or will a drier and more stable \nsouthwesterly flow develop? Looking at the spectrum of model\nsolutions both scenarios are possible. We'll know more a time \ndraws nearer.",
